<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>(a) <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are both on the meridian <m>37^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m>. <m>P</m> is north of the equator and <m>Q</m> is south of it. The distance <m>PQ</m> along the meridian is <m>{360}</m> nm. Give a possible latitude for <m>P</m> and for <m>Q</m>.</p><p><m>P</m>: <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/> <m>^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m>, <m>Q</m>: <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> <m>^\circ\,\mathrm{S}</m>   </p><p>(b) <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are both on the meridian <m>37^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m> and both north of the equator, <m>{840}</m> nm apart. Give possible latitudes.</p><p><m>R</m>: <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/> <m>^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m>, <m>S</m>: <fillin characters="5" name="ans4"/> <m>^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m>   </p><p>There are many correct answers. Decimals are allowed.</p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Along a meridian, <m>1</m> nm is <m>1'</m> of latitude, so <m>{360}</m> nm is <m>{360}' = {6}^\circ</m>. <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are on opposite sides of the equator, so their latitudes add to <m>{6}^\circ</m>: for example <m>P</m> at <m>2^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m> and <m>Q</m> at <m>{4}^\circ\,\mathrm{S}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>{840}</m> nm is <m>{14}^\circ</m>. <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are on the same side, so their latitudes differ by <m>{14}^\circ</m>: for example <m>10^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m> and <m>{24}^\circ\,\mathrm{N}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>4</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>24</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>10</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>